  "query_preview": "..\nToday: Winds remain breezy along the Cascade gaps early this\nmorning in the wake of a shortwave trough that made its way\nacross the region. While winds will continue to ease through \nthe morning hours, expect another round of breezy westerly winds\nto develop this afternoon and evening as another shortwave \ntraverses the region. This will again lead to elevated to \nlocally critical fire weather conditions in the Wenatchee area \nand the Waterville Plateau with gusts around 20-35 mph and \nrelative humidities primarily between 15-25 percent. The \nwildcard remains smoke reducing temperatures in those areas, \nwhich would help reduce fire risk. Elsewhere across the area, \nexpect temperatures to cool a few degrees today under the \ninfluence of zonal flow aloft and another passing shortwave. \nWildfire smoke and haze will continue to impact the region, \nespecially in the vicinity of or downwind of ongoing wildfires.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Zonal flow will continue through at least \nTuesday, bringing near normal temperatures to the region and \nbreezy winds to the Cascade gaps in the late afternoons and \nevenings. \n\nWednesday through Sunday: Confidence in the forecast decreases \nheading into the second half of the week as a broad shortwave \ntrough sinks down from British Columbia. This system may bring the \nfirst chance of precipitation in a few weeks to northern areas near \nthe Canadian border by Wednesday. While there is a 15-25% chance\nof showers, preciptiation looks to remain rather limited with \nthis system. Small chances of (10-20%) of thunderstorms remain\npossible in northeast WA and northern ID both Wednesday and \nThursday afternoon and evenings. Quasi-zonal flow will then \nbriefly return on Friday as the trough to the north exits the \narea. Ensemble guidance remains indicative of an upper level \ntrough developing along the coast over the weekend and bringing\nsouthwest flow aloft to the area. This could continue to raise \nfurther concerns for thunderstorms across the region by the \nweekend.",
